A scroll's `direction` reaches the runtime through the set gtk4.sngl
declares for it, `_set_Scroll_direction`, which calls
`cgo.SnglScrollSetDirection` with the axis as a Go string; the wrapper hands
it to `sngl_scroll_set_direction`, so the program holds no C string. That
sets the scrolled window's two policies, lays the content out at its natural
size along each axis that scrolls, and reads @reachEnd along x in a scroll
moving sideways.

A scroll given no direction is set "vertical", ui.ScrollDirection's default,
which is how a scroll SnglScrollNew makes already moves.
deny out/go/gtk4/main.go `import "C"` -- the direction crosses as a Go string through the generated wrapper, so the program imports no C
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        gtk4
    }
}

ui.window(title="t") {
    ui.scroll #down {
        ui.text(value="rows")
    }
    ui.scroll #across(direction=horizontal) {
        ui.text(value="columns")
    }
    ui.scroll #either(direction=both) {
        ui.text(value="a page")
    }
}
